 "resolution_2026-09-16": {
  "hyphen_defect": "CAUSE FIXED IN THE ENGINE, DATA UNCHANGED. The engine no longer refuses these: '-' is the older Wylie/ACIP spelling of the prefix mark EWTS writes '.', and both are read now (5c3d77f8). 38 of the 38 sampled entries recorded here render correctly today; 0 still do not, and those carry a SEPARATE unreadable token (a '~~' corpus placeholder, or 'lva'), which is flagged rather than guessed at. The 664 stored Tibetan values still hold a literal U+002D and are still wrong; correcting them is the data project's, not this repo's — CLAUDE.md: the app imports a release, it does not own the data.",
  "wa_zur_v": "RESOLVED, AND IT WAS NOT WHAT THIS FILE GUESSED. This section said the difference needed a reader because the headwords spell the sound with 'v'. They do, and 'v' is simply not EWTS: V is the ACIP code for wa-zur, and acip_to_ewts was passing it through unmapped, so the derived wylie was never valid in the first place. Fixed in both engines 2026-09-16 — a V with a letter before it becomes 'w'; a V that opens a syllable is left alone. Measured on the spine: 1,615 segments carry a subjoined V, none rendered without a flag, 1,113 do now; corpus-wide 35,791 -> 36,904 of 42,199. This is gap 2 of docs/FINDING_ACIP_EWTS_GAPS.md. The stored Tibetan for these headwords still spells wa-zur as BA + TSA-PHRU where Illuminator writes SUBJOINED WA, and that remains a data question for the data project. WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX, stated plainly: all 16 of the entries listed above STILL flag, and correctly so. Their stored wylie is 'grva', 'dvangs', 'rtsva' — 'v' baked into the master's wylie column by the old converter — and 'v' is not EWTS, so refusing it is the engine doing its job. The fix converts ACIP as it is read, which is what the corpus, the Overlay and the drill pack go through; it cannot reach a column that was already written. Those 16 headwords need the data project to re-derive their wylie.",
  "still_open": "Gap 3, the visarga ':', is untouched. GA-YAS is still not understood and still not guessed at. A separate defect surfaced while measuring: engines/ewts_unicode.py RAISES KeyError on 8 spine rows where an invalid character strands a Sanskrit final in onset position, while the C++ port correctly flags them — the oracle is the broken one, and any Python-side tooling over the corpus dies on those rows.",
  "note": "engine_says values in the sections above were recorded before the fixes and have been re-run; each entry now carries engine_said_when_filed alongside engine_says_now."
 },
 "stva_cluster_2026-09-17": {
  "what": "Our engine and the Illuminator disagree on the Sanskrit stva cluster, and our own ground truth is silent.",
  "ours": "wylie_to_unicode('stwaM') gives སྟྭཾ = 0F66 0F9F 0FAD 0F7E — sa with a SUBJOINED ta, then wa-zur. One stack.",
  "illuminator": "Writes སཏྭ = 0F66 0F4F 0FAD — sa FULL, then ta with wa-zur — in all 10 of its records for the cluster, including bodhi satva, stva re, dgra stva, satva. Its own transliteration of སཏྭ is 'stva', so it maps the same input string to a different rendering.",
  "why_it_is_not_being_changed": "The difference is syllabification: sa-tva as two units against stva as one stack. Which is right depends on what the source text means by the ACIP STVAm, and this project's own ground truth cannot say — ZERO of the 105,634 master entries touch the cluster, so there is no attested pair to measure against. Changing a canonical engine on one external source, against no internal evidence, is guessing with a citation attached. Filed for a reader with Sanskrit competence, or for Geshe Michael to rule.",
  "reach": "14 corpus segments contain STVAm.",
  "found_by": "the visarga audit's adversarial lens, 2026-09-16; checked against the Illuminator and the master 2026-09-17."
 }
